(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/doc_type "adr")
  (:adr/id "adr-2609110100-payload-v3-has-no-representation-blocker-only-a-conversion-contract")
  (:adr/topic "gap-02-payload-v3-tag-coverage")
  (:adr/title "Payload v3 has no representation blocker: all twelve normalize tags round-trip through kotoba.value.v1")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-10")
  (:adr/deciders (vector "Jun Kawasaki" "Claude Opus 5"))
  (:adr/repos (vector "com-junkawasaki/root" "kotoba-lang/osaho" "kotoba-lang/io-ipld"))
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2609076000-kir-is-an-ipld-codec-schema-and-adl"))
  (:adr/body
  "## Context

ADR-2609076000 decision 1 put io-ipld into osaho as a TEST-ONLY dependency and
made promotion to :deps the payload v3 decision. The cost side was already
measured: all ten frozen vectors disagree with kotoba.value.v1 along three
dimensions (tag alphabet, integer representation, map ordering).

The half that had not been measured is whether v3 is POSSIBLE. If any tag that
osaho's normalize emits has no representation in kotoba.value.v1, then v3 is
not a decision at all -- it waits on an io-ipld extension, and that extension is
a blocker with a name.

## Measurement

The twelve tags are read off osaho's kotoba.kir.definition-identity/normalize
on origin/main rather than from memory: nil bool int str kw sym bytes f64 map
set vec list.

Reading io-ipld's code table and counting thirteen codes (0-7, 9, 16-19, where 8
is link) is a WEAKER claim than it appears. A code can exist while its encoder
rejects the shape osaho actually produces, and osaho does not produce bare
numbers -- it renders integers and floats as decimal STRINGS inside its
normalize output. So the measurement round-trips a representative value per tag
through kotoba.value.codec/encode-value and decode-value.

Reproduce from origin/main worktrees, not the shared checkouts:

    git -C orgs/kotoba-lang/osaho    worktree add --detach WT origin/main
    git -C orgs/kotoba-lang/io-ipld  worktree add --detach WT kotoba-lang/main

The second remote is NOT called origin. west checkouts name the remote after
the org, so `origin/main` does not resolve in io-ipld and the worktree add
fails with `invalid reference`. Run the probe from the io-ipld worktree:

    nbb --classpath src:io-multiformats/src:text/src:org-ietf-cbor/src:org-nist-sha2/src \\
        gap02-tag-coverage.cljs

Five repositories are needed on the classpath and only one of them (text) is
named in io-ipld's nbb.edn; the rest surface one at a time as
`Could not find namespace`.

## Result: thirteen of thirteen, with four discriminating controls

Every tag round-trips, including the i64 wrapper carrying a value beyond the
JavaScript safe-integer range. Four controls are rejected, each for a DIFFERENT
named reason -- :value/unwrapped-number, :value/record-unsupported,
:value/float-nan, :value/int64-not-an-exact-integer -- so the probe is not one
that passes everything put in front of it.

**There is no representation blocker. Payload v3 is possible.**

What v3 costs is therefore a CONVERSION CONTRACT, not an upstream extension.
The three known differences stay exactly as measured, and each is now known to
be a mapping rather than a gap: osaho's twelve lowercase string tags against
v1's numeric codes; osaho's decimal-string numbers against v1's required Int64
and Float64 wrapper records; and the two map orderings. v1 also carries one code
osaho's normalize never emits -- link, code 8 -- so in that direction v1 is a
superset.

## The first run reported a false blocker, and the shape of that error matters

The first version of this probe passed a bare 1.5 and reported
`f64 REJECTED :value/unwrapped-number`, which reads exactly like the named
blocker this measurement was looking for. It is not one. The codec's own
rejection carries the hint `wrap a float with ipld.value/float64` -- the
rejection was of the probe's shape, not of the tag.

This is the failure this workspace already names: the red that comes from
breaking it the wrong way looks like a successful demonstration. It was caught
only because the reason literal was printed next to the verdict and turned out
to be the SAME literal the NaN control produced -- two rows that should have
failed for different reasons failing for the same one. A probe that had recorded
only pass and fail would have shipped the false blocker.

The bare float is therefore kept as a CONTROL rather than deleted, so the
wrapper is what discriminates, and the mistake cannot quietly return.

A second probe bug was found the same way: the cases were built eagerly, so a
constructor rejection escaped the try and killed the whole run instead of being
recorded as one row. Cases are thunks now.

## Consequences and what is next

- v3 is a decision about conversion and blast radius, not about capability.
  Nothing is waiting on io-ipld.
- The unmeasured half of gap 02 remains: the blast radius of a moving DefCID --
  how many DefCIDs are baked into locks, manifests and cache keys. That count,
  not representability, is what decides whether v3 is affordable.
- This ADR does not promote io-ipld to :deps. It removes one candidate reason
  for not doing so.")))
